# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in this tree is LF, in git and in the working copy. That was
# true by accident until now, which let a tooling slip rewrite 13 files as CRLF
# and turn a 179-line change into an 11,788-line one. This file makes it a rule.
#
# eol=lf rather than plain text=auto: the working copy gets LF on Windows too.
# MSVC, rc.exe and PowerShell all read LF without complaint.
#
# The line above used to end "That is already the state on disk, so this introduces
# no churn." That was false when it was written, and stayed false for five spans,
# because eol=lf acts only at CHECKOUT -- it cannot rewrite a file already sitting
# in the working copy. Measured 2026-09-15: 17 of 189 tracked text files still held
# CRLF on disk, src/input.cpp and src/editor.cpp entirely so. Nothing could report
# it, because the INDEX was already normalised, so `git status` was clean and
# `git diff` empty; the only way to see it was to count \r\n bytes.
#
# Two things that made it worth fixing rather than just noting. Three multi-line
# raw string literals in src/startpage.cpp (kSampleDocument, kMermaidTour,
# kMarkdownBasics) embedded their line endings, so those documents were 171 bytes
# longer in my working copy than in the commit -- a local build compiled different
# string contents than a fresh clone, and no test asserts on them. And normalising
# the working copy cannot change what is committed: the 17 files then showed up in
# `git status` only as a stale stat cache, `git add` staged 0 rows and 0 bytes, and
# HEAD was untouched.
#
# So the rule works and only its self-description was wrong -- which is the most
# believable kind of wrong claim, sitting in accurate, load-bearing text that was
# verified once.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Images must never be touched. text=auto guesses correctly on these today, but a
# guess is not a guarantee and a single normalised byte corrupts them.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
